        "original_id": "d46098e1-2031-57d1-a401-0cc0b59cdb9b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Dysteleology, argument from poor design, dysteleological argument, science-oriented atheism\n- People: Ernst Haeckel, Richard Dawkins, Sam Harris, Christopher Hitchens\n- Publications: New Statesman, The Nation, The Weekly Standard, The Atlantic, London Review of Books, The Times Literary Supplement, Slate, Free Inquiry, Vanity Fair\n\nFacts:\n- Dysteleology is an aggressive but optimistic form of science-oriented atheism.\n- Dysteleology was originally perhaps associated with Ernst Haeckel and his followers.\n- Dysteleology is now perhaps more associated with the atheism of Richard Dawkins, Sam Harris, and Christopher Hitchens.\n- Christopher Hitchens contributed to New Statesman, The Nation, The Weekly Standard, The Atlantic, London Review of Books, The Times Literary Supplement, Slate, Free Inquiry, and Vanity Fair.\n- The argument from poor design is also known as the dysteleological argument.\n- The argument from poor design argues against the existence of a creator God.\n- The argument from poor design reasons that an omnipotent and omnibenevolent God would not create organisms with perceived suboptimal designs seen in nature.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
